  user_input {
    user_response: "# PROJECT: MRITIDHRISTI \342\200\223 PREVENT \342\200\242 DETECT \342\200\242 PROTECT\n\nAct as a senior software architect, full-stack developer, UI/UX designer, AI/ML engineer, healthcare technology specialist and offline-first application developer.\n\nYour task is to DESIGN AND BUILD a complete, integrated, functional, premium-quality healthcare web application prototype called:\n\n**MRITIDHRISTI \342\200\223 PREVENT \342\200\242 DETECT \342\200\242 PROTECT**\n\nDo not create merely a landing page, static dashboard or UI mockup. Build a complete, modular application with working navigation, functional forms, persistent data, backend integration and interconnected workflows.\n\nThe application must demonstrate all three pillars as one unified healthcare platform.\n\n---\n\n# 1. PROJECT VISION\n\nMritidhristi is an integrated, offline-first, AI-assisted healthcare support platform that connects:\n\n1. PREVENT \342\200\223 Early identification of Type 2 diabetes lifestyle risk and long-term prevention support.\n2. DETECT \342\200\223 AI-assisted diabetic retinopathy screening for individuals already diagnosed with diabetes.\n3. PROTECT \342\200\223 Doctor review, referrals, appointments, clinical follow-up and continuity of care.\n\nIt also includes:\n- Offline-first operation.\n- AI/ML integration.\n- Explainable AI.\n- Human clinical oversight.\n- Patient tracking.\n- District-level healthcare resource planning using MATLAB Simulink.\n\nIMPORTANT CLINICAL BOUNDARY:\n\nThis is a healthcare screening and support prototype. It is not a diagnostic replacement. Lifestyle risk assessment cannot diagnose diabetes. Retinal AI predictions cannot replace ophthalmologist assessment. All clinical decisions remain with authorised healthcare professionals.\n\n---\n\n# 2. TECHNOLOGY ARCHITECTURE\n\nUse a maintainable, modular architecture.\n\nFrontend:\n- React\n- TypeScript\n- Vite\n- Tailwind CSS\n- Recharts\n- Lucide icons\n\nBackend:\n- Python FastAPI\n- REST APIs\n- Pydantic validation\n\nDatabase:\n- PostgreSQL\n- SQLAlchemy ORM\n- Database migrations\n\nAI/ML:\n- Python\n- PyTorch\n- OpenCV\n- Scikit-learn where appropriate\n\nOffline:\n- Progressive Web App\n- Service Worker\n- IndexedDB\n- Dexie.js\n- Offline action queue\n- Synchronisation engine\n\nSimulation:\n- MATLAB Simulink\n- Documented input/output integration\n\nSecurity:\n- Role-based access control\n- Secure authentication\n- Password hashing\n- Session management\n- Audit logging\n- Encryption in transit and at rest\n\nIf an external service, database or API requires credentials, create configuration placeholders and document setup. Never expose secrets in frontend code.\n\n---\n\n# 3. PREMIUM UI/UX DESIGN\n\nCreate a visually stunning, professional healthcare SaaS platform.\n\nDesign direction:\n- Deep navy blue.\n- Teal.\n- White.\n- Subtle green.\n- Soft neutral backgrounds.\n- Elegant typography.\n- Rounded cards.\n- Subtle shadows.\n- Clean data visualisations.\n- Professional medical dashboard aesthetic.\n\nThe design must feel like a polished real-world healthcare product.\n\nCreate:\n- Responsive desktop and mobile interfaces.\n- Collapsible sidebar.\n- Top navigation.\n- Global search where appropriate.\n- Notifications.\n- User profile.\n- Connectivity status.\n- Synchronisation status.\n- Consistent reusable components.\n- Loading, empty, success and error states.\n\nAvoid clutter, excessive gradients and unnecessary animations.\n\nThe UI should be accessible and readable.\n\n---\n\n# 4. USER ROLES\n\nCreate three role-based application experiences.\n\nA. PATIENT\n- Personal dashboard.\n- Lifestyle risk assessment.\n- Prevention programme.\n- Habit tracking.\n- Retinal screening status.\n- Reports.\n- Appointments.\n- Notifications.\n- Follow-up history.\n\nB. DOCTOR / HEALTHCARE WORKER\n- Patient overview.\n- Risk assessment history.\n- Retinal screening reports.\n- AI-generated screening assistance.\n- Image visualisation.\n- Clinical review.\n- Referral management.\n- Appointment tracking.\n- Follow-up management.\n- Patient history.\n\nC. DISTRICT ADMINISTRATOR\n- District-level analytics.\n- Patient statistics.\n- Prevention programme statistics.\n- Screening statistics.\n- Referral statistics.\n- Healthcare resource utilisation.\n- Simulation results.\n- Operational insights.\n\nImplement role-based navigation and permissions. Do not merely hide buttons; enforce permissions on backend APIs too.\n\n---\n\n# 5. PILLAR ONE \342\200\223 PREVENT\n\nBuild a complete Type 2 diabetes lifestyle risk and prevention module.\n\n## A. Patient registration\n- Basic demographic details.\n- Consent.\n- Language preference.\n- Patient profile.\n\nSupport English and Tamil as primary languages. Keep the architecture ready for Hindi.\n\n## B. Nine-input lifestyle assessment\n\nCollect:\n\n1. Age.\n2. Diet.\n3. Sleep.\n4. Physical activity and daily steps.\n5. Weight.\n6. Waist circumference.\n7. Family history.\n8. Smoking.\n9. Alcohol consumption.\n\nUse a user-friendly questionnaire with one question at a time, icons, simple descriptions and progress indication.\n\n## C. Risk assessment\n\nDisplay:\n- Risk category.\n- Contributing factors.\n- Top three modifiable risk factors.\n- Explanation of the result.\n- Recommendations.\n\nUse a clearly documented scoring method only when its validity is established. Otherwise label the prototype assessment as an experimental or rule-based lifestyle screening score.\n\nDo not invent a clinically validated prediction model.\n\n## D. What-if analysis\n\nAllow users to modify appropriate lifestyle inputs and observe how the experimental risk score changes.\n\nClearly explain that this is an illustrative scenario, not a medical prediction.\n\n## E. Clinical testing guidance\n\nProvide appropriate guidance about:\n- HbA1c.\n- Fasting plasma glucose.\n\nAllow authorised healthcare professionals to record available laboratory results and interpret them according to applicable clinical guidelines.\n\nNever infer confirmed diabetes from lifestyle inputs alone.\n\n## F. Twelve-month prevention programme\n\nDivide it into:\n\nPHASE 1: LEARNING \342\200\223 Months 1\342\200\2236.\nPHASE 2: MAINTENANCE \342\200\223 Months 7\342\200\22312.\n\nFeatures:\n- Personalised Indian food guidance.\n- Activity recommendations.\n- Sleep improvement.\n- Weekly educational lessons.\n- Daily habit tracking.\n- Weekly check-ins.\n- Progress charts.\n- Coach/health-worker support.\n- 30-day, 60-day and 90-day checkpoints.\n- Continued monitoring throughout months 4\342\200\22312.\n- Weight and waist progress.\n- HbA1c/FPG tracking when available.\n\n## G. Adaptive intervention system\n\nImplement a deterministic rule-based intervention ladder.\n\nExamples:\n- Missed weekly check-in.\n- Ask about barriers.\n- Offer simpler alternative activities.\n- Adjust reminders.\n- Escalate to coach.\n- Escalate concerning symptoms or clinical measurements to a clinician.\n\nKeep experimental contextual-bandit intervention selection separate from clinical decision-making. It may select only pre-approved educational messages and intervention options.\n\n---\n\n# 6. PILLAR TWO \342\200\223 DETECT\n\nBuild a complete AI-assisted diabetic retinopathy screening module.\n\nThis module is for individuals already diagnosed with diabetes.\n\n## A. Retinal image acquisition\n\nCreate:\n- Image upload.\n- Camera/image source information.\n- Image preview.\n- Patient and image association.\n- Upload validation.\n- Screening history.\n\n## B. Seven-step retinal image-processing and reporting pipeline\n\nThe seven stages begin AFTER image acquisition.\n\nSTEP 1: IMAGE QUALITY ASSESSMENT\n- Blur detection.\n- Brightness assessment.\n- Exposure checks.\n- Image resolution validation.\n- Ungradable image rejection.\n- Recapture recommendation.\n\nSTEP 2: IMAGE ENHANCEMENT AND PREPROCESSING\n- Retinal image cropping.\n- Illumination correction.\n- CLAHE.\n- Denoising.\n- Normalisation.\n- Preprocessing preview.\n\nSTEP 3: RETINAL ANATOMICAL STRUCTURE DETECTION\n- Optic disc.\n- Retinal vessels.\n- Macula and relevant anatomical regions.\n\nSTEP 4: LESION DETECTION\n- Hard exudates.\n- Haemorrhages.\n- Other supported lesion categories.\n- Microaneurysms as an advanced capability.\n- Clearly distinguish implemented and experimental detection.\n\nSTEP 5: CNN-BASED DR GRADING\n- ResNet/EfficientNet model integration.\n- Five diabetic retinopathy severity classes using the ICDR framework.\n- Training and inference pipeline.\n- Model evaluation.\n\nSTEP 6: GRAD-CAM EXPLAINABILITY\n- Generate visual explanations from the actual model.\n- Overlay activation heatmaps.\n- Allow comparison with the original image.\n- Explain limitations of Grad-CAM.\n\nSTEP 7: ANNOTATED REPORT GENERATION\n- Patient information.\n- Image-quality result.\n- Model output.\n- Confidence/uncertainty.\n- Visual explanation.\n- Human-review status.\n- Screening summary.\n\n## C. Dataset integration\n\nPrepare dataset loaders and documentation for:\n\n1. APTOS 2019 \342\200\223 primary DR grading experiments.\n2. IDRiD \342\200\223 Indian retinal images, DR grading and lesion segmentation.\n3. DRIVE \342\200\223 retinal vessel segmentation and anatomical analysis.\n4. Messidor-2 \342\200\223 external evaluation where access permits.\n\nNever treat these datasets as interchangeable.\n\nImplement:\n- Dataset validation.\n- Label mapping.\n- Preprocessing.\n- Patient-level split where identifiers allow.\n- Leakage prevention.\n- Training.\n- Validation.\n- Testing.\n- External evaluation.\n- Metrics: sensitivity, specificity, AUC, precision, recall and F1.\n\nDo not fabricate training results, model accuracy, clinical validation or model weights.\n\nIf datasets are not supplied, create the pipeline and sample demonstration mode with clearly labelled synthetic or illustrative outputs.\n\n## D. AI reliability and safety\n\nImplement:\n- Image-quality rejection.\n- Prediction uncertainty handling.\n- Out-of-distribution detection strategy.\n- Conflicting result handling.\n- Human review fallback.\n- Safe withholding of unreliable grades.\n\nA low-confidence or unreliable prediction must not be presented as a confirmed grade.\n\nClearly mark AI results as screening assistance requiring clinical interpretation.\n\n---\n\n# 7. PILLAR THREE \342\200\223 PROTECT\n\nBuild a complete doctor-led clinical coordination module.\n\n## A. Doctor dashboard\n\nDisplay:\n- Patient details.\n- Medical history.\n- Lifestyle assessment.\n- Laboratory results where available.\n- Retinal images.\n- AI screening results.\n- Grad-CAM.\n- Previous reports.\n- Referral history.\n- Follow-up status.\n\n## B. Clinical review\n\nSupport:\n- Doctor review.\n- Confirmation or rejection of AI-assisted findings.\n- Clinical notes.\n- Recommended next steps.\n- Triage categories.\n\nTriage must follow clinician-approved protocols. Never base urgency solely on AI confidence or lifestyle risk.\n\n## C. Referral management\n\n- Generate referral records.\n- Assign healthcare facility.\n- Record referral reason.\n- Track referral status.\n- Record specialist findings.\n- Track completion.\n\n## D. Appointment management\n\n- Appointment scheduling.\n- Appointment confirmation status.\n- Attendance.\n- Cancellation.\n- Missed-appointment tracking.\n- Follow-up reminders.\n\n## E. Continuity of care\n\n- Assign health-worker ownership.\n- Track patient follow-up.\n- Escalate missed appointments.\n- Record specialist feedback.\n- Schedule the next screening.\n- Maintain longitudinal patient history.\n\nAll three pillars must use shared patient records.\n\n---\n\n# 8. OFFLINE-FIRST FUNCTIONALITY\n\nOffline operation is a core requirement, not an optional visual feature.\n\nImplement genuine offline capabilities.\n\nA. PWA\n- Installable application.\n- App shell caching.\n- Service Worker.\n- Offline status indicator.\n\nB. IndexedDB\nUse Dexie.js to store permitted local data:\n- Patient records needed for assigned workflows.\n- Prevention progress.\n- Locally completed assessments.\n- Pending actions.\n- Cached permitted reports.\n\nC. Offline operations\nDemonstrate:\n- Offline access to cached application pages.\n- Offline lifestyle assessment.\n- Offline habit tracking.\n- Offline recording of supported patient workflow actions.\n- Pending action queue.\n- Clear pending/synchronised status.\n\nD. Synchronisation\n- Detect restored connectivity.\n- Upload queued changes.\n- Validate changes on the backend.\n- Handle duplicate submissions.\n- Resolve conflicts.\n- Preserve audit history.\n- Show synchronisation errors.\n\nE. Security\n- Avoid insecure localStorage for sensitive health records.\n- Use appropriate local data protection.\n- Apply role-based access.\n- Minimise locally cached patient information.\n- Provide session protection.\n\nClearly identify features that cannot function offline, including remote doctor review, external appointment confirmation and cloud-based updates.\n\nCreate a demonstrable offline test workflow.\n\n---\n\n# 9. DATABASE DESIGN\n\nDesign a proper relational database with tables/entities for:\n\n- Users.\n- Roles.\n- Patients.\n- Consent records.\n- Lifestyle assessments.\n- Risk assessment results.\n- Laboratory results.\n- Prevention programmes.\n- Weekly lessons.\n- Habit logs.\n- Intervention records.\n- Retinal images.\n- Image-quality assessments.\n- Model predictions.\n- Model versions.\n- Grad-CAM records.\n- Screening reports.\n- Doctor reviews.\n- Referrals.\n- Appointments.\n- Follow-ups.\n- Notifications.\n- Audit logs.\n- Offline synchronisation records.\n- District resources.\n- Simulation scenarios.\n- Simulation results.\n\nDefine:\n- Primary keys.\n- Foreign keys.\n- Relationships.\n- Constraints.\n- Timestamps.\n- Appropriate indexes.\n\nCreate migrations and sample synthetic records.\n\n---\n\n# 10. DISTRICT RESOURCE PLANNING \342\200\223 SIMULINK\n\nCreate an administrator-facing simulation module.\n\nIt must represent district-level healthcare capacity, not patient-level diagnosis.\n\nInclude:\n- Patient arrivals.\n- Camera availability.\n- Screening capacity.\n- Image-processing throughput.\n- Ophthalmologist availability.\n- Health-worker workload.\n- Referral demand.\n- Queue lengths.\n- Waiting times.\n- Resource utilisation.\n- Estimated operational costs.\n\nImplement six scenarios:\n\n1. Normal conditions.\n2. High patient volume.\n3. Limited connectivity.\n4. Camera shortage.\n5. Ophthalmologist shortage.\n6. Increased referral demand.\n\nCreate a documented interface for importing actual Simulink outputs, such as CSV or a supported API.\n\nIf MATLAB/Simulink is unavailable, provide an explicitly labelled illustrative simulation mode. Do not claim it is an actual Simulink execution.\n\nShow scenario comparisons and charts.\n\n---\n\n# 11. SECURITY AND PRIVACY\n\nImplement:\n- Secure authentication.\n- Role-based authorisation.\n- Password hashing.\n- Patient consent.\n- Session expiry.\n- Input validation.\n- API protection.\n- Audit trails.\n- Safe file upload validation.\n- Secure configuration.\n- Environment variables.\n- Backup guidance.\n\nUse synthetic patient data for demonstrations. Do not expose real patient information or credentials.\n\nTreat regulatory compliance as a requirement to be verified, not a claim to be automatically achieved.\n\n---\n\n# 12. DASHBOARD ANALYTICS\n\nCreate meaningful dashboards.\n\nPATIENT:\n- Lifestyle risk history.\n- Prevention progress.\n- Habit completion.\n- Upcoming appointments.\n- Screening status.\n\nDOCTOR:\n- Patients requiring review.\n- Unreviewed screening results.\n- Referral status.\n- Missed appointments.\n- Follow-up workload.\n\nADMINISTRATOR:\n- Registered patients.\n- Assessments completed.\n- Screening throughput.\n- Image-quality rejection rate.\n- Referrals.\n- Waiting time.\n- District resource utilisation.\n- Simulation comparisons.\n\nClearly distinguish real application records from sample and simulated data.\n\n---\n\n# 13. IMPLEMENTATION AND QUALITY REQUIREMENTS\n\nThis project must be modular, maintainable and testable.\n\nRequirements:\n- Reusable components.\n- Separate frontend/backend/AI/offline modules.\n- API validation.\n- Error handling.\n- Form validation.\n- Loading states.\n- Empty states.\n- Success and failure notifications.\n- Responsive design.\n- Database persistence.\n- Unit tests.\n- Integration tests.\n- Documentation.\n- Setup instructions.\n- Environment configuration.\n- Seed data scripts.\n\nNever:\n- Create buttons that do nothing.\n- Use static fake results as real AI predictions.\n- Pretend offline functionality works without testing it.\n- Invent clinical accuracy.\n- Invent completed referrals or appointments.\n- Claim Simulink has run when it has not.\n- Hide incomplete features.\n\nFor unavailable integrations, create clean interfaces and clearly labelled placeholders.\n\n---\n\n# 14. DEVELOPMENT PROCESS\n\nBuild the project in phases.\n\nPHASE 1:\nArchitecture, project setup, design system, navigation and role-based dashboards.\n\nPHASE 2:\nPREVENT module.\n\nPHASE 3:\nDETECT module.\n\nPHASE 4:\nPROTECT module.\n\nPHASE 5:\nDatabase and backend integration.\n\nPHASE 6:\nOffline-first functionality.\n\nPHASE 7:\nSimulink integration.\n\nPHASE 8:\nEnd-to-end testing, debugging and documentation.\n\nDo not attempt to generate everything in one uncontrolled operation.\n\nAfter each phase:\n1. List files created and modified.\n2. Explain the implementation.\n3. Run the application and tests.\n4. Identify incomplete functionality.\n5. Fix errors.\n6. Document how to verify the features.\n7. Preserve the existing working implementation.\n\nMaintain a project progress document showing:\n- Implemented features.\n- Partially implemented features.\n- Simulated features.\n- Experimental features.\n- Pending features.\n\n---\n\n# FINAL EXPECTATION\n\nDeliver a complete, polished, integrated Mritidhristi prototype that demonstrates a connected journey:\n\nPATIENT REGISTRATION \342\206\222 PREVENTION ASSESSMENT \342\206\222 CLINICAL TESTING GUIDANCE \342\206\222 RETINAL SCREENING \342\206\222 AI-ASSISTED REVIEW \342\206\222 DOCTOR DECISION \342\206\222 REFERRAL \342\206\222 APPOINTMENT \342\206\222 FOLLOW-UP.\n\nAll three pillars must work together through shared patient records.\n\nThe result must be suitable for technical inspection, live demonstration and further development in Google Antigravity.\n\nBegin with PHASE 1. Inspect the available environment, explain the architecture briefly and start implementation. Do not stop after creating a design or plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nMedia attachment: User sent attachment: /assets/aistudio/image_dffbe40e.png (saved in workspace at /public/assets/aistudio/image_dffbe40e.png). Read it with view_file at that path; in shell commands, which run from the workspace root, use the relative path public/assets/aistudio/image_dffbe40e.png. When displaying or embedding this asset in code, reference this local file path directly. Only invoke image generation tools if the user explicitly requests creating or modifying an image.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    items {
      text: "# PROJECT: MRITIDHRISTI \342\200\223 PREVENT \342\200\242 DETECT \342\200\242 PROTECT\n\nAct as a senior software architect, full-stack developer, UI/UX designer, AI/ML engineer, healthcare technology specialist and offline-first application developer.\n\nYour task is to DESIGN AND BUILD a complete, integrated, functional, premium-quality healthcare web application prototype called:\n\n**MRITIDHRISTI \342\200\223 PREVENT \342\200\242 DETECT \342\200\242 PROTECT**\n\nDo not create merely a landing page, static dashboard or UI mockup. Build a complete, modular application with working navigation, functional forms, persistent data, backend integration and interconnected workflows.\n\nThe application must demonstrate all three pillars as one unified healthcare platform.\n\n---\n\n# 1. PROJECT VISION\n\nMritidhristi is an integrated, offline-first, AI-assisted healthcare support platform that connects:\n\n1. PREVENT \342\200\223 Early identification of Type 2 diabetes lifestyle risk and long-term prevention support.\n2. DETECT \342\200\223 AI-assisted diabetic retinopathy screening for individuals already diagnosed with diabetes.\n3. PROTECT \342\200\223 Doctor review, referrals, appointments, clinical follow-up and continuity of care.\n\nIt also includes:\n- Offline-first operation.\n- AI/ML integration.\n- Explainable AI.\n- Human clinical oversight.\n- Patient tracking.\n- District-level healthcare resource planning using MATLAB Simulink.\n\nIMPORTANT CLINICAL BOUNDARY:\n\nThis is a healthcare screening and support prototype. It is not a diagnostic replacement. Lifestyle risk assessment cannot diagnose diabetes. Retinal AI predictions cannot replace ophthalmologist assessment. All clinical decisions remain with authorised healthcare professionals.\n\n---\n\n# 2. TECHNOLOGY ARCHITECTURE\n\nUse a maintainable, modular architecture.\n\nFrontend:\n- React\n- TypeScript\n- Vite\n- Tailwind CSS\n- Recharts\n- Lucide icons\n\nBackend:\n- Python FastAPI\n- REST APIs\n- Pydantic validation\n\nDatabase:\n- PostgreSQL\n- SQLAlchemy ORM\n- Database migrations\n\nAI/ML:\n- Python\n- PyTorch\n- OpenCV\n- Scikit-learn where appropriate\n\nOffline:\n- Progressive Web App\n- Service Worker\n- IndexedDB\n- Dexie.js\n- Offline action queue\n- Synchronisation engine\n\nSimulation:\n- MATLAB Simulink\n- Documented input/output integration\n\nSecurity:\n- Role-based access control\n- Secure authentication\n- Password hashing\n- Session management\n- Audit logging\n- Encryption in transit and at rest\n\nIf an external service, database or API requires credentials, create configuration placeholders and document setup. Never expose secrets in frontend code.\n\n---\n\n# 3. PREMIUM UI/UX DESIGN\n\nCreate a visually stunning, professional healthcare SaaS platform.\n\nDesign direction:\n- Deep navy blue.\n- Teal.\n- White.\n- Subtle green.\n- Soft neutral backgrounds.\n- Elegant typography.\n- Rounded cards.\n- Subtle shadows.\n- Clean data visualisations.\n- Professional medical dashboard aesthetic.\n\nThe design must feel like a polished real-world healthcare product.\n\nCreate:\n- Responsive desktop and mobile interfaces.\n- Collapsible sidebar.\n- Top navigation.\n- Global search where appropriate.\n- Notifications.\n- User profile.\n- Connectivity status.\n- Synchronisation status.\n- Consistent reusable components.\n- Loading, empty, success and error states.\n\nAvoid clutter, excessive gradients and unnecessary animations.\n\nThe UI should be accessible and readable.\n\n---\n\n# 4. USER ROLES\n\nCreate three role-based application experiences.\n\nA. PATIENT\n- Personal dashboard.\n- Lifestyle risk assessment.\n- Prevention programme.\n- Habit tracking.\n- Retinal screening status.\n- Reports.\n- Appointments.\n- Notifications.\n- Follow-up history.\n\nB. DOCTOR / HEALTHCARE WORKER\n- Patient overview.\n- Risk assessment history.\n- Retinal screening reports.\n- AI-generated screening assistance.\n- Image visualisation.\n- Clinical review.\n- Referral management.\n- Appointment tracking.\n- Follow-up management.\n- Patient history.\n\nC. DISTRICT ADMINISTRATOR\n- District-level analytics.\n- Patient statistics.\n- Prevention programme statistics.\n- Screening statistics.\n- Referral statistics.\n- Healthcare resource utilisation.\n- Simulation results.\n- Operational insights.\n\nImplement role-based navigation and permissions. Do not merely hide buttons; enforce permissions on backend APIs too.\n\n---\n\n# 5. PILLAR ONE \342\200\223 PREVENT\n\nBuild a complete Type 2 diabetes lifestyle risk and prevention module.\n\n## A. Patient registration\n- Basic demographic details.\n- Consent.\n- Language preference.\n- Patient profile.\n\nSupport English and Tamil as primary languages. Keep the architecture ready for Hindi.\n\n## B. Nine-input lifestyle assessment\n\nCollect:\n\n1. Age.\n2. Diet.\n3. Sleep.\n4. Physical activity and daily steps.\n5. Weight.\n6. Waist circumference.\n7. Family history.\n8. Smoking.\n9. Alcohol consumption.\n\nUse a user-friendly questionnaire with one question at a time, icons, simple descriptions and progress indication.\n\n## C. Risk assessment\n\nDisplay:\n- Risk category.\n- Contributing factors.\n- Top three modifiable risk factors.\n- Explanation of the result.\n- Recommendations.\n\nUse a clearly documented scoring method only when its validity is established. Otherwise label the prototype assessment as an experimental or rule-based lifestyle screening score.\n\nDo not invent a clinically validated prediction model.\n\n## D. What-if analysis\n\nAllow users to modify appropriate lifestyle inputs and observe how the experimental risk score changes.\n\nClearly explain that this is an illustrative scenario, not a medical prediction.\n\n## E. Clinical testing guidance\n\nProvide appropriate guidance about:\n- HbA1c.\n- Fasting plasma glucose.\n\nAllow authorised healthcare professionals to record available laboratory results and interpret them according to applicable clinical guidelines.\n\nNever infer confirmed diabetes from lifestyle inputs alone.\n\n## F. Twelve-month prevention programme\n\nDivide it into:\n\nPHASE 1: LEARNING \342\200\223 Months 1\342\200\2236.\nPHASE 2: MAINTENANCE \342\200\223 Months 7\342\200\22312.\n\nFeatures:\n- Personalised Indian food guidance.\n- Activity recommendations.\n- Sleep improvement.\n- Weekly educational lessons.\n- Daily habit tracking.\n- Weekly check-ins.\n- Progress charts.\n- Coach/health-worker support.\n- 30-day, 60-day and 90-day checkpoints.\n- Continued monitoring throughout months 4\342\200\22312.\n- Weight and waist progress.\n- HbA1c/FPG tracking when available.\n\n## G. Adaptive intervention system\n\nImplement a deterministic rule-based intervention ladder.\n\nExamples:\n- Missed weekly check-in.\n- Ask about barriers.\n- Offer simpler alternative activities.\n- Adjust reminders.\n- Escalate to coach.\n- Escalate concerning symptoms or clinical measurements to a clinician.\n\nKeep experimental contextual-bandit intervention selection separate from clinical decision-making. It may select only pre-approved educational messages and intervention options.\n\n---\n\n# 6. PILLAR TWO \342\200\223 DETECT\n\nBuild a complete AI-assisted diabetic retinopathy screening module.\n\nThis module is for individuals already diagnosed with diabetes.\n\n## A. Retinal image acquisition\n\nCreate:\n- Image upload.\n- Camera/image source information.\n- Image preview.\n- Patient and image association.\n- Upload validation.\n- Screening history.\n\n## B. Seven-step retinal image-processing and reporting pipeline\n\nThe seven stages begin AFTER image acquisition.\n\nSTEP 1: IMAGE QUALITY ASSESSMENT\n- Blur detection.\n- Brightness assessment.\n- Exposure checks.\n- Image resolution validation.\n- Ungradable image rejection.\n- Recapture recommendation.\n\nSTEP 2: IMAGE ENHANCEMENT AND PREPROCESSING\n- Retinal image cropping.\n- Illumination correction.\n- CLAHE.\n- Denoising.\n- Normalisation.\n- Preprocessing preview.\n\nSTEP 3: RETINAL ANATOMICAL STRUCTURE DETECTION\n- Optic disc.\n- Retinal vessels.\n- Macula and relevant anatomical regions.\n\nSTEP 4: LESION DETECTION\n- Hard exudates.\n- Haemorrhages.\n- Other supported lesion categories.\n- Microaneurysms as an advanced capability.\n- Clearly distinguish implemented and experimental detection.\n\nSTEP 5: CNN-BASED DR GRADING\n- ResNet/EfficientNet model integration.\n- Five diabetic retinopathy severity classes using the ICDR framework.\n- Training and inference pipeline.\n- Model evaluation.\n\nSTEP 6: GRAD-CAM EXPLAINABILITY\n- Generate visual explanations from the actual model.\n- Overlay activation heatmaps.\n- Allow comparison with the original image.\n- Explain limitations of Grad-CAM.\n\nSTEP 7: ANNOTATED REPORT GENERATION\n- Patient information.\n- Image-quality result.\n- Model output.\n- Confidence/uncertainty.\n- Visual explanation.\n- Human-review status.\n- Screening summary.\n\n## C. Dataset integration\n\nPrepare dataset loaders and documentation for:\n\n1. APTOS 2019 \342\200\223 primary DR grading experiments.\n2. IDRiD \342\200\223 Indian retinal images, DR grading and lesion segmentation.\n3. DRIVE \342\200\223 retinal vessel segmentation and anatomical analysis.\n4. Messidor-2 \342\200\223 external evaluation where access permits.\n\nNever treat these datasets as interchangeable.\n\nImplement:\n- Dataset validation.\n- Label mapping.\n- Preprocessing.\n- Patient-level split where identifiers allow.\n- Leakage prevention.\n- Training.\n- Validation.\n- Testing.\n- External evaluation.\n- Metrics: sensitivity, specificity, AUC, precision, recall and F1.\n\nDo not fabricate training results, model accuracy, clinical validation or model weights.\n\nIf datasets are not supplied, create the pipeline and sample demonstration mode with clearly labelled synthetic or illustrative outputs.\n\n## D. AI reliability and safety\n\nImplement:\n- Image-quality rejection.\n- Prediction uncertainty handling.\n- Out-of-distribution detection strategy.\n- Conflicting result handling.\n- Human review fallback.\n- Safe withholding of unreliable grades.\n\nA low-confidence or unreliable prediction must not be presented as a confirmed grade.\n\nClearly mark AI results as screening assistance requiring clinical interpretation.\n\n---\n\n# 7. PILLAR THREE \342\200\223 PROTECT\n\nBuild a complete doctor-led clinical coordination module.\n\n## A. Doctor dashboard\n\nDisplay:\n- Patient details.\n- Medical history.\n- Lifestyle assessment.\n- Laboratory results where available.\n- Retinal images.\n- AI screening results.\n- Grad-CAM.\n- Previous reports.\n- Referral history.\n- Follow-up status.\n\n## B. Clinical review\n\nSupport:\n- Doctor review.\n- Confirmation or rejection of AI-assisted findings.\n- Clinical notes.\n- Recommended next steps.\n- Triage categories.\n\nTriage must follow clinician-approved protocols. Never base urgency solely on AI confidence or lifestyle risk.\n\n## C. Referral management\n\n- Generate referral records.\n- Assign healthcare facility.\n- Record referral reason.\n- Track referral status.\n- Record specialist findings.\n- Track completion.\n\n## D. Appointment management\n\n- Appointment scheduling.\n- Appointment confirmation status.\n- Attendance.\n- Cancellation.\n- Missed-appointment tracking.\n- Follow-up reminders.\n\n## E. Continuity of care\n\n- Assign health-worker ownership.\n- Track patient follow-up.\n- Escalate missed appointments.\n- Record specialist feedback.\n- Schedule the next screening.\n- Maintain longitudinal patient history.\n\nAll three pillars must use shared patient records.\n\n---\n\n# 8. OFFLINE-FIRST FUNCTIONALITY\n\nOffline operation is a core requirement, not an optional visual feature.\n\nImplement genuine offline capabilities.\n\nA. PWA\n- Installable application.\n- App shell caching.\n- Service Worker.\n- Offline status indicator.\n\nB. IndexedDB\nUse Dexie.js to store permitted local data:\n- Patient records needed for assigned workflows.\n- Prevention progress.\n- Locally completed assessments.\n- Pending actions.\n- Cached permitted reports.\n\nC. Offline operations\nDemonstrate:\n- Offline access to cached application pages.\n- Offline lifestyle assessment.\n- Offline habit tracking.\n- Offline recording of supported patient workflow actions.\n- Pending action queue.\n- Clear pending/synchronised status.\n\nD. Synchronisation\n- Detect restored connectivity.\n- Upload queued changes.\n- Validate changes on the backend.\n- Handle duplicate submissions.\n- Resolve conflicts.\n- Preserve audit history.\n- Show synchronisation errors.\n\nE. Security\n- Avoid insecure localStorage for sensitive health records.\n- Use appropriate local data protection.\n- Apply role-based access.\n- Minimise locally cached patient information.\n- Provide session protection.\n\nClearly identify features that cannot function offline, including remote doctor review, external appointment confirmation and cloud-based updates.\n\nCreate a demonstrable offline test workflow.\n\n---\n\n# 9. DATABASE DESIGN\n\nDesign a proper relational database with tables/entities for:\n\n- Users.\n- Roles.\n- Patients.\n- Consent records.\n- Lifestyle assessments.\n- Risk assessment results.\n- Laboratory results.\n- Prevention programmes.\n- Weekly lessons.\n- Habit logs.\n- Intervention records.\n- Retinal images.\n- Image-quality assessments.\n- Model predictions.\n- Model versions.\n- Grad-CAM records.\n- Screening reports.\n- Doctor reviews.\n- Referrals.\n- Appointments.\n- Follow-ups.\n- Notifications.\n- Audit logs.\n- Offline synchronisation records.\n- District resources.\n- Simulation scenarios.\n- Simulation results.\n\nDefine:\n- Primary keys.\n- Foreign keys.\n- Relationships.\n- Constraints.\n- Timestamps.\n- Appropriate indexes.\n\nCreate migrations and sample synthetic records.\n\n---\n\n# 10. DISTRICT RESOURCE PLANNING \342\200\223 SIMULINK\n\nCreate an administrator-facing simulation module.\n\nIt must represent district-level healthcare capacity, not patient-level diagnosis.\n\nInclude:\n- Patient arrivals.\n- Camera availability.\n- Screening capacity.\n- Image-processing throughput.\n- Ophthalmologist availability.\n- Health-worker workload.\n- Referral demand.\n- Queue lengths.\n- Waiting times.\n- Resource utilisation.\n- Estimated operational costs.\n\nImplement six scenarios:\n\n1. Normal conditions.\n2. High patient volume.\n3. Limited connectivity.\n4. Camera shortage.\n5. Ophthalmologist shortage.\n6. Increased referral demand.\n\nCreate a documented interface for importing actual Simulink outputs, such as CSV or a supported API.\n\nIf MATLAB/Simulink is unavailable, provide an explicitly labelled illustrative simulation mode. Do not claim it is an actual Simulink execution.\n\nShow scenario comparisons and charts.\n\n---\n\n# 11. SECURITY AND PRIVACY\n\nImplement:\n- Secure authentication.\n- Role-based authorisation.\n- Password hashing.\n- Patient consent.\n- Session expiry.\n- Input validation.\n- API protection.\n- Audit trails.\n- Safe file upload validation.\n- Secure configuration.\n- Environment variables.\n- Backup guidance.\n\nUse synthetic patient data for demonstrations. Do not expose real patient information or credentials.\n\nTreat regulatory compliance as a requirement to be verified, not a claim to be automatically achieved.\n\n---\n\n# 12. DASHBOARD ANALYTICS\n\nCreate meaningful dashboards.\n\nPATIENT:\n- Lifestyle risk history.\n- Prevention progress.\n- Habit completion.\n- Upcoming appointments.\n- Screening status.\n\nDOCTOR:\n- Patients requiring review.\n- Unreviewed screening results.\n- Referral status.\n- Missed appointments.\n- Follow-up workload.\n\nADMINISTRATOR:\n- Registered patients.\n- Assessments completed.\n- Screening throughput.\n- Image-quality rejection rate.\n- Referrals.\n- Waiting time.\n- District resource utilisation.\n- Simulation comparisons.\n\nClearly distinguish real application records from sample and simulated data.\n\n---\n\n# 13. IMPLEMENTATION AND QUALITY REQUIREMENTS\n\nThis project must be modular, maintainable and testable.\n\nRequirements:\n- Reusable components.\n- Separate frontend/backend/AI/offline modules.\n- API validation.\n- Error handling.\n- Form validation.\n- Loading states.\n- Empty states.\n- Success and failure notifications.\n- Responsive design.\n- Database persistence.\n- Unit tests.\n- Integration tests.\n- Documentation.\n- Setup instructions.\n- Environment configuration.\n- Seed data scripts.\n\nNever:\n- Create buttons that do nothing.\n- Use static fake results as real AI predictions.\n- Pretend offline functionality works without testing it.\n- Invent clinical accuracy.\n- Invent completed referrals or appointments.\n- Claim Simulink has run when it has not.\n- Hide incomplete features.\n\nFor unavailable integrations, create clean interfaces and clearly labelled placeholders.\n\n---\n\n# 14. DEVELOPMENT PROCESS\n\nBuild the project in phases.\n\nPHASE 1:\nArchitecture, project setup, design system, navigation and role-based dashboards.\n\nPHASE 2:\nPREVENT module.\n\nPHASE 3:\nDETECT module.\n\nPHASE 4:\nPROTECT module.\n\nPHASE 5:\nDatabase and backend integration.\n\nPHASE 6:\nOffline-first functionality.\n\nPHASE 7:\nSimulink integration.\n\nPHASE 8:\nEnd-to-end testing, debugging and documentation.\n\nDo not attempt to generate everything in one uncontrolled operation.\n\nAfter each phase:\n1. List files created and modified.\n2. Explain the implementation.\n3. Run the application and tests.\n4. Identify incomplete functionality.\n5. Fix errors.\n6. Document how to verify the features.\n7. Preserve the existing working implementation.\n\nMaintain a project progress document showing:\n- Implemented features.\n- Partially implemented features.\n- Simulated features.\n- Experimental features.\n- Pending features.\n\n---\n\n# FINAL EXPECTATION\n\nDeliver a complete, polished, integrated Mritidhristi prototype that demonstrates a connected journey:\n\nPATIENT REGISTRATION \342\206\222 PREVENTION ASSESSMENT \342\206\222 CLINICAL TESTING GUIDANCE \342\206\222 RETINAL SCREENING \342\206\222 AI-ASSISTED REVIEW \342\206\222 DOCTOR DECISION \342\206\222 REFERRAL \342\206\222 APPOINTMENT \342\206\222 FOLLOW-UP.\n\nAll three pillars must work together through shared patient records.\n\nThe result must be suitable for technical inspection, live demonstration and further development in Google Antigravity.\n\nBegin with PHASE 1. Inspect the available environment, explain the architecture briefly and start implementation. Do not stop after creating a design or plan.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_START]\nMedia attachment: User sent attachment: /assets/aistudio/image_dffbe40e.png (saved in workspace at /public/assets/aistudio/image_dffbe40e.png). Read it with view_file at that path; in shell commands, which run from the workspace root, use the relative path public/assets/aistudio/image_dffbe40e.png. When displaying or embedding this asset in code, reference this local file path directly. Only invoke image generation tools if the user explicitly requests creating or modifying an image.\n[AIS_METADATA_SECTION_END]"
    }
